{"id":"valid-palindrome-ii","title":"Valid Palindrome II","difficulty":"Easy","pattern":"Strings","tags":["Two Pointers","String","Greedy"],"companies":{"google":2,"apple":2,"meta":5,"amazon":2,"microsoft":2},"description":"Given a string `s`, return `true` if it is a palindrome, or can be turned into one by deleting **at most one** character. Otherwise return `false`.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"aba\"","output":"true","explanation":"Already a palindrome; no deletion needed."},{"input":"s = \"abca\"","output":"true","explanation":"Delete the b (giving aca) or the c (giving aba)."},{"input":"s = \"abc\"","output":"false","explanation":"Removing any single character leaves a two-letter string with different letters."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length <= 10^5","s consists of lowercase English letters"],"hints":["Use two pointers from both ends as you would to check an ordinary palindrome.","At the first mismatch you have exactly two choices: drop the left character or drop the right one. Each choice leaves a plain palindrome check on the remaining range.","You never need to branch twice: a second mismatch means that choice failed, so the total work is linear."],"function":"validPalindrome","signature":"def validPalindrome(self, s: str) -> bool:","arg_types":{"s":"str"},"return_type":"bool","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"aba"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"abca"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"abc"},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"a"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"ab"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"abcda"},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"deeee"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"eedede"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"abcdefdcba"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"abcdeedcbx"},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"cbbcc"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":""},"output":true},{"input":{"s":""},"output":false}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def validPalindrome(self, s: str) -> bool:\n        def is_pal(i: int, j: int) -> bool:\n            while i < j:\n                if s[i] != s[j]:\n                    return False\n                i += 1; j -= 1\n            return True\n\n        i, j = 0, len(s) - 1\n        while i < j:\n            if s[i] != s[j]:\n                return is_pal(i + 1, j) or is_pal(i, j - 1)\n            i += 1; j -= 1\n        return True\n","approach":"**Key insight: the first mismatch forces the decision.** Characters that matched from the outside in are already correct; at the first `s[i] != s[j]`, the only way to fix things with one deletion is to drop `s[i]` or `s[j]`. Each option reduces to a plain palindrome check on the remaining range, with no further deletions allowed. Try both; if either succeeds, answer true.\n\nTwo linear scans at most, O(1) extra space. The greedy is sound because deleting a character outside the mismatching pair cannot help: those outer pairs already matched and would be thrown out of alignment.\n\n**Common mistakes:** recursing with the deletion budget and re-checking from the very start (O(n^2)); using slicing like `s[i+1:j+1][::-1]` which is correct but allocates O(n) strings; checking only one of the two options; forgetting that an already-palindromic string is valid without any deletion.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Generalise to at most k deletions: the two-way branch becomes a DP equal to `n - longest palindromic subsequence <= k`, O(n^2). Return *which* character to delete. Streaming or very long inputs: the two-pointer approach needs random access, so discuss what changes if the data only arrives sequentially.","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["valid-palindrome","longest-palindromic-subsequence","longest-palindrome"],"number":187,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def validPalindrome(self, s: str) -> bool:\n        pass\n"}